You are writing an independent pytest test file for a Python module you have
never seen the implementation of. Write tests strictly from the public
contract and the acceptance criteria below. Do not guess internal helpers.

## Public contract (this is all you get — no implementation source)

```python
from dataclasses import dataclass, field

@dataclass
class Section:
    """One row of the section tree.

    `level` is 0 for the document root and for the special `(front matter)`
    and `(preamble)` rows, and 1-6 for real Markdown headings (H1-H6).
    `own_text` is the section's own content, excluding its children's text.
    """
    title: str
    level: int
    own_text: str
    children: list["Section"] = field(default_factory=list)


def parse_sections(text: str) -> Section:
    """Parse `text` (a full Markdown document) into a Section tree.

    Returns the document root (title="(document)", level=0, own_text="").
    Its children, in document order, are: an optional `(front matter)` row
    (if `text` starts with a `---`-delimited YAML block), an optional
    `(preamble)` row (text between the front matter, or the start of the
    file, and the first heading), and then the real heading sections,
    nested by level (a heading's children are the headings that follow it
    at a deeper level, until a heading at the same or a shallower level
    ends it).
    """
```

## The behavior that changed (this PBI's whole point)

Two things about `own_text` changed from an earlier version of this module,
and both are being fixed **at the same time**, verified by one strong
invariant:

**The invariant every test should lean on:** walk the tree in a pre-order,
document-order traversal (front matter first if present, then preamble if
present, then each heading section before its own children, children before
following siblings) and concatenate every visited node's `own_text`
(including the root's, which is always `""`). That concatenation must
reconstruct the **original source text byte-for-byte** — nothing missing,
nothing duplicated, nothing added.

This single invariant is what catches both fixes:

1. **A heading section's `own_text` now includes its own heading line**
   (e.g. for `"## Title\ncontent\n"`, that section's `own_text` is the
   whole thing, `"## Title\ncontent\n"` — starting at the `#`, not at the
   line after it). Previously the heading line itself belonged to no
   section's `own_text` anywhere in the tree, so the full-document
   concatenation was short by exactly the heading lines' text.
2. **The front matter's `own_text` now absorbs the blank line(s) that
   immediately follow its closing `---`** (so `"---\nk: v\n---\n\n# H1\n"`
   puts `"---\nk: v\n---\n\n"` — including that blank line — in the front
   matter section, not in a separate `(preamble)` node holding just
   `"\n"`). Previously that blank line ended up in its own `(preamble)`
   node, which is a second way the tree failed to line up with the raw
   text when read section-by-section in isolation (the concatenation
   invariant above still reconstructs the text either way, but a
   `(preamble)` node that is nothing but whitespace should no longer
   appear at all when it would only ever hold that absorbed blank line —
   see AC-04 below).

## Test cases to write, one test (or more) per item

- **AC-01**: Parse `"# Only Title\nSome body text.\n"` (a single heading, no
  front matter, no preamble). Find the section with `title == "Only Title"`
  and assert its `own_text` **starts with** `"# Only Title"` (the heading
  line is included), and that the invariant holds: concatenating the root's
  `own_text` plus every child's `own_text` in document order equals the
  original string exactly.

- **AC-02**: Parse a document with **several headings that have no body at
  all between them** — this is the real-world case that motivated this fix
  (a Markdown file where `#` lines are used like shell comments, each
  followed immediately by another `#` line):

  ```
  # First comment line
  # Second comment line
  # Third comment line
  # Real heading with body
  Some actual content here.
  ```

  Assert that **none** of the four resulting H1 sections has an empty or
  whitespace-only `own_text` — each one's `own_text` is at minimum its own
  heading line, so `section.own_text.strip()` is non-empty for all four,
  even the three that have no body content of their own.

- **AC-03**: Parse a document with YAML front matter followed by a heading,
  with **one blank line** between the closing `---` and the heading:

  ```
  ---
  key: value
  ---

  # Heading
  Body text.
  ```

  (as a Python string:
  `"---\nkey: value\n---\n\n# Heading\nBody text.\n"`). Assert the
  concatenation invariant holds (front matter `own_text` + heading
  `own_text`, in order, equals the original string exactly) — this passes
  whether the blank line ends up inside the front matter node or in a
  separate `(preamble)` node, **but** also assert directly: there is
  **no** child of the root with `level == 0` and `title == "(preamble)"`
  whose `own_text.strip() == ""` — a preamble row that holds nothing but
  whitespace must not exist as a separate node when that whitespace was
  only the gap after the front matter's closing `---`.

- **AC-04**: Parse a longer document with front matter, a preamble
  paragraph with **real, non-blank text**, and two headings at different
  levels:

  ```
  ---
  title: "x"
  ---
  This is a real preamble paragraph, not blank.

  # H1
  H1 body.

  ## H2
  H2 body.
  ```

  Assert: a `(preamble)` section exists and its `own_text` contains the
  real preamble sentence (`"real preamble paragraph"` appears in it); the
  `H1` section's `own_text` starts with `"# H1"`; the `H2` section's
  `own_text` starts with `"## H2"` and `H2` is a child of `H1` (nested, per
  the existing level-nesting rule — this part of the contract is
  unchanged); and the full concatenation invariant holds for the whole
  document.

- **AC-05**: Parse a document with **no** front matter and **no** blank
  line issue at all — just two sibling H1 headings, each with body text —
  and assert the concatenation invariant holds. This is the plain,
  unremarkable case: it must keep working exactly as before.

  ```
  # First
  First body.

  # Second
  Second body.
  ```

## Output format

Return exactly one fenced Python code block, nothing else: no prose before
or after it. The file must be self-contained pytest. Include a helper that
walks the tree in the documented pre-order (front matter, preamble, then
headings before children before following siblings) and concatenates every
node's `own_text` — you will need it for the invariant in every test. Start
with:

```python
from tokmd.sections import Section, parse_sections
```

Test function names must start with `test_` and should reference the AC id
they check in a comment, e.g. `# AC-01`.
